  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 201 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nThursday, a longwave trough will become established across the \nwestern United States and Pacific Northwest as a negative PNA \npattern begins to develop. Downstream of this trough, upper-level \nridging will amplify across the central United States, resulting in \nincreasing 500-mb heights and generally westerly flow aloft across \nthe region. With the ridge remaining the dominant feature across the \ncentral and northern Plains, increasing subsidence should support \nmainly dry conditions through the day Thursday. Warmer temperatures \nwithin the lower to middle levels will also support afternoon highs \nin the middle to upper 60s across much of the area. With surface \nhigh pressure remaining nearby, winds should remain relatively \nlight, with overnight lows generally in the middle to upper 40s.\nFriday, a southern-stream shortwave disturbance is forecast to \napproach the region ahead of the broader longwave trough across the \nwestern United States. Increasing southeasterly low-level flow will \nsupport moisture advection into the central Plains, while increasing \nmid- and upper-level moisture accompanies the approaching \ndisturbance. Recent model guidance has begun to show a stronger \nsignal for precipitation development as increasing moisture overlaps \nwith forcing for ascent ahead of the shortwave. At this time, \nprecipitation appears more likely to remain scattered in coverage, \nwith the better potential favoring central and eastern Nebraska. \nHowever, uncertainty remains regarding how far west precipitation \nmay develop, and trends will continue to be monitored over the next \nseveral forecast cycles. Highs Friday should remain in the lower to \nmiddle 60s, with overnight lows generally in the upper 30s to middle \n40s.\n\nSaturday into Sunday, the western longwave trough will gradually \nbegin progressing eastward, particularly by late weekend, resulting \nin strengthening southwesterly flow aloft across the central Plains. \nWith increased moisture remaining across the region, subtle \ndisturbances embedded within the southwesterly flow may provide \nadditional opportunities for precipitation through the weekend. \nHowever, confidence in the timing, coverage, and location of \nprecipitation remains low given continued differences among model \nguidance.\n\nBy early next week, attention turns toward the potential for a more \norganized storm system as the western trough begins ejecting \neastward into the central United States. Both the ECMWF/EPS and \nGFS/GEFS suites generally support the development of a closed upper-\nlevel low, though considerable differences remain regarding its \neventual track, timing, and intensity. The ECMWF/EPS suite currently \nfavors a more northerly track, which could bring the stronger \nforcing for ascent farther north across the central and northern \nPlains. In contrast, the GFS/GEFS suite favors a more southerly \nevolution, potentially shifting the axis of more widespread \nprecipitation farther south and east. Given the relatively amplified \nand blocky upper-level pattern, uncertainty remains high regarding \nhow quickly the system will progress eastward and where the greatest \nprecipitation potential will ultimately develop. Nevertheless, \nconfidence is gradually increasing in a more active pattern by early \nnext week, with the potential for more widespread precipitation as \nthe upper-level system approaches. Temperatures should remain \ngenerally in the lower to middle 60s through much of the weekend, \nwith a cooling trend possible early next week as the storm system \nmoves into the region. Highs may fall into the 40s and 50s, though \nthe magnitude and timing of the cooling will depend on the eventual \ntrack and evolution of the system.",
